    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: on successful owner create, emit a line via the logger named `NOTIFY` containing the owner id and the memberId.\n\n**Implementation** \u2014 one file, one method, minimal structural impact:\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline already ends with a `logAudit` step (`LogAuditOnCreate`) that runs only on a successful create and already holds the persisted `Owner` \u2014 with both its id and its `customerCode` (the value exposed as `memberId`, per `OwnerMapper`). I added a dedicated `NOTIFY` logger and a single welcome line there:\n\n```java\nprivate static final Logger NOTIFY = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"NOTIFY\");\n...\nNOTIFY.info(\"welcome owner id={} memberId={}\", owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode());\n```\n\n**Why this keeps cost low:** `LogAuditOnCreate` is a single-method class (WMC_other \u2248 0), the edited method stays CC 1, and only that one existing step is touched \u2014 no new function class and no YAML rewiring, so exactly **1 file** is touched. The alternative (a new `EnqueueWelcome` step class plus editing `owners.POST.yml`) would touch 2 files and add a fresh function's worth of lines, costing several times more under the given model.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest.coreEnqueuesWelcome` check passes: the `NOTIFY` capture sees `welcome owner id=11 memberId=NSW2716E6609E1`, containing both the id and the memberId read back from `GET /api/owners/{id}`.",
